<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Spartacus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 71 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Spartacus was a Thracian gladiator who led the largest slave revolt against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a>, the Third Servile War of 73–71 BCE. Escaping from a gladiator school at Capua with about seventy companions, he gathered an army of tens of thousands and defeated several Roman forces across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. He was killed in battle against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-licinius-crassus">Marcus Licinius Crassus</a> in 71 BCE, and thousands of captured rebels were crucified along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appian-way">Appian Way</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/spartacus.jpg" alt="Spartacus" width="320" height="552" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carole Raddato from FRANKFURT, Germany · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%CE%A3%CF%80%CE%AC%CF%81%CF%84%CE%B1%CE%BA%CE%BF%CF%82_Spartacus,Espartaco_marble_statue_in_Louvre_%22%CE%9C%CE%B1%CE%B9%CE%B4%CE%BF%CE%B9_clan_-Meadow%22_slave_macedon-greek.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>No contemporary sources for Spartacus exist. The accounts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> and Appian were written more than a century after his death, by people who were not involved, and they contradict one another. Plutarch calls him a Thracian of nomadic stock, possibly of the Maedi tribe, who lived in what is now south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. Appian says he had once served as a soldier with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, but had since been a prisoner and was sold as a gladiator.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> adds that his wife, a prophetess of the Maedi, was enslaved with him. One modern author estimates that he was about 30 when the revolt began. He was trained as a murmillo at the gladiatorial school of Lentulus Batiatus near Capua.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 73 BCE about 70 slaves plotted to escape the school. They seized kitchen utensils, fought their way out, took wagons of gladiatorial weapons and defeated the soldiers sent after them. They retired to Mount Vesuvius and chose Spartacus and two Gallic slaves, Crixus and Oenomaus, as leaders. The Roman legions were busy with a revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-mithridatic-war">Third Mithridatic War</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> first sent a militia under the praetor Gaius Claudius Glaber. Spartacus's men climbed down the mountain on ropes of vines and attacked the camp from behind. Slaves, herdsmen and shepherds then flocked to the rebels, swelling their numbers to some 70,000.</p><p>In 72 BCE the Senate sent two consular legions, which beat a group of 30,000 rebels under Crixus and were then defeated by Spartacus. The Senate then gave command of eight legions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-licinius-crassus">Marcus Licinius Crassus</a>. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a>, Spartacus bargained with Cilician pirates to carry some 2,000 men to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, but they betrayed him. Crassus fortified the isthmus at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reggio-calabria">Rhegium</a>, and the rebels broke through and marched towards Brundusium.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In the final battle in 71 BCE, which one account places in the High Sele Valley in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucania">Lucania</a>, the rebels were routed and most were killed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a>, Appian and Florus claim that Spartacus died in it, though Appian adds that his body was never found. Six thousand captured survivors were crucified along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appian-way">Appian Way</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to Capua. The ancient historians disagree about his aims: Plutarch says he wanted to escape over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> and send his men home, while Appian and Florus say he intended to march on Rome. None of his actions suggests that he aimed to abolish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>.</p><p>In later centuries he became a symbol of resistance. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a> called the Third Servile War the only just war in history, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a> admired him. The German Spartacus League took his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Spartacus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Slave revolts in Sicily from 135 BCE and the rising of <b>Spartacus</b> in 73–71 BCE showed its dangers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Earlier, the Thracian gladiator <b>Spartacus</b> had led a great slave rising in southern Italy in 73–71 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/culture/thracians">Thracians · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>It recovered as a Roman colony and later saw the slave revolt of <b>Spartacus</b> begin in its gladiator school in 73 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/capua">Capua</a></li><li><blockquote>Some scholars read Plutarch as saying that <b>Spartacus</b> was a Maedian.</blockquote><a href="/state/maedi">Maedi</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucania">Lucania</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42,26&amp;year=-81" aria-label="Thrace on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5182 668 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Spartacus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5632 938h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5632 938h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42,26&amp;year=-81">What was here in 81 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
